%I A400149 #37 Nov 02 2026 17:18:31
%S A400149 1,1,2,3,5,9,17,29,56,106,202,384
%N A400149 Number of distinct multisets {k_1, ..., k_r}, 3 <= k_i <= n, such that some n-set has subsets of sizes k_1, ..., k_r with any two of them sharing at most one element.
%C A400149 In other words, a(n) is the number of ways n lines could meet if the only rule were that two lines meet at most once: the subsets are the sets of lines through the points where at least three lines meet, and two such points cannot share two lines. Equivalently, a(n) is the number of distinct multisets of sizes of the lines with at least three points in a linear space on n points (a simple matroid of rank at most 3).
%C A400149 The empty multiset is counted, so a(1) = a(2) = 1.
%C A400149 a(n) <= A072964(n-1), the number of multisets with Sum_{i=1..r} binomial(k_i,2) <= binomial(n,2); equality holds only for n <= 3 (for n = 4 the multiset {3, 3} satisfies the inequality but two 3-subsets of a 4-set share two elements).
%C A400149 A399464(n) counts the multisets that occur for arrangements of straight lines in the real plane. A399464(n) = a(n) for n <= 6 and A399464(n) < a(n) for 7 <= n <= 12. The first multiset counted here but not in A399464 is {3^7} at n = 7 (the Fano plane); it is realizable by straight lines for n >= 8.
%C A400149 The terms were computed by an exhaustive search over linear spaces with prescribed line sizes and confirmed independently by a SAT solver for n <= 12.
%D A400149 B. Grünbaum, Arrangements and Spreads, CBMS Regional Conference Series in Mathematics 10, AMS, Providence, 1972.
%H A400149 Petra Radko, <a href="/A400149/a400149.txt">Table of the a(n) multisets for n <= 12</a>
%H A400149 Petra Radko, <a href="https://gwynerva.github.io/line-pencils/sequences/combinatorial/">How n Lines Can Meet: combinatorially possible types</a>, 2026.
%H A400149 Petra Radko, <a href="https://github.com/Gwynerva/line-pencils">Programs, logs and verification notes</a>, GitHub, 2026.
%e A400149 For n = 4 the a(4) = 3 multisets are {}, {3} and {4}.
%e A400149 For n = 7 the a(7) = 17 multisets include {3^7} (seven 3's), realized by the Fano plane.
%Y A400149 Cf. A072964, A001200, A399464, A399605.
%K A400149 nonn,more,new
%O A400149 1,3
%A A400149 _Petra Radko_, Oct 25 2026
